Marc, this was super informative--thank you for the excellent summary. Really helped me cut through the fog after my reading on this.

After I posted this question I've learned of the 'spending degrees of freedom' approach where only one model is developed for a given data set and question (Giudice et al. 2012. Spending degrees of freedom in a poor economy: a case study of building a sightability model for moose in northeastern Minnesota. J. Wildl. Mgt. 76(1):75-87).  And so it bypasses any issues related to model averaging.  They stress a priori data-reduction techniques and thinking hard about which variables really matter.  They point out that concepts for this approach apply to multi-model approaches as well (effective sample size, screening predictors, collinearity, overly influential observations, etc).  Anyway this paper was also helpful for me.

Thanks again Marc.

Mike

